\details{
Survey data reports the state at each wave, while the walk reads a panel
layer as a list of updates applied in time order. \code{prepare_dynes_data()}
rewrites the named panel layers into that change-list form, so the state the
walk builds at every wave equals the survey.

Two facts are declared per layer and never detected, because
survey-recorded interactions repeated at each wave are a legitimate shape
that a heuristic would misread:
\itemize{
\item the \strong{style} (\code{layers}): the process the layer records.
\code{"mutually_exclusive"} is a binary tie that is created and dissolved,
\code{"count"} is one monotone process of repeated events, and \code{"none"} is a
valued layer with no process, typically a covariate.
\item the \strong{form} (\code{form}): what a value at a wave means. \code{"states"} (the
default) is the level at the wave, \code{"per_wave"} is the amount since the
previous wave, and \code{"changes"} means the rows already are a change-list
in the layer's \code{update} semantics.
}

A binary panel modeled as a process, such as a friendship survey, takes
\code{"mutually_exclusive"}; a count panel, such as calls tallied at each wave,
takes \code{"count"}; a valued covariate takes \code{"none"}. A covariate stored as
raw survey rows needs the same rewrite as a modeled layer, since otherwise
every dropped tie stays in the network.

A tie that is present or absent has no amount since the previous wave, so
\code{"mutually_exclusive"} cannot be combined with \code{"per_wave"}. A
\code{"mutually_exclusive"} layer leaves the function flavored, with the map
\code{\link[=add_flavor]{add_flavor()}} records under \code{flavor_style = "mutually_exclusive"}, so there
is no separate flavoring step. A \code{"count"} layer needs no flavor: it is
estimated as a count panel. A layer that counts two event types together
should be split into one \code{"count"} layer per type.
}
\section{Rows read and written}{

Of a \code{"states"} or \code{"per_wave"} layer's timed rows, only the time, sender,
receiver and value are read. A wave holds one row per dyad, so an \code{order}
column has nothing to order and is not carried onto the written rows. Each
changed dyad gets one row per wave in the layer's own \code{update} semantics:
the new level under \code{"replace"} (0 for a dissolution), the difference under
\code{"increment"}. The \code{time = NA} rows and the rows of layers not named are
kept as they are.

The respondent set is yours to choose before the call. An actor missing
from a wave loses all their ties there, written as dissolutions like any
dropped tie.
}

\section{Waves and the observation start}{

Every wave is written at its own time, the first as its difference from the
layer's \code{time = NA} rows (or from the empty network when there are none).
No row is moved to \code{time = NA}, and the observation start is set once, in
\code{set_preprocessing(start_time = )}. Rows at or before the start are applied
in time order and never scored, so a fit started at a later wave enters with
that wave's survey as its state.

Under the default start, \code{\link[=estimate_dynes]{estimate_dynes()}} starts at the first wave: the
first survey is the initial state, and the rows written for it are history,
never augmented. A \code{start_time} before the first survey makes the first
survey an interval endpoint instead of the initial state: the \code{time = NA}
rows are the state that interval enters with, and the first wave's changes
are augmented as latent events within it.
}

